        <section id="software-methodologies"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Software methodologies</h2>
              <p>There are many different methodologies and frameworks used in project management for software projects.  In this course we are going to focus on four - Agile, Scrum, Kanban, and Waterfall.</p>
<p>Select the images below to see about the four different software methodologies.</p>

              
              <!-- IF 2 --><ul class="l-icon-grid ">
  
  
    
    <!--  -->
      
  
  
    
  
  <li class="c-reveal-block">
    <img class="c-reveal-block__icon js-showhide" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u1_s1_p3_reveal1.svg" alt="Agile" width="266" height="266">
    
    <div class="c-reveal-block__desc">
      <h3 aria-hidden="true">Agile</h3>
      <p>Agile is an iterative, people-oriented and result-focused way of working when delivering software products. The software is built incrementally in time-boxed 'sprints'.</p>

    </div>
  </li>
      
  
  
    
  
  <li class="c-reveal-block">
    <img class="c-reveal-block__icon js-showhide" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u1_s1_p3_reveal2.svg" alt="Scrum" width="266" height="266">
    
    <div class="c-reveal-block__desc">
      <h3 aria-hidden="true">Scrum</h3>
      <p>Scrum is an Agile methodology often called a framework to help teams to work together on a project. Scrum is quick and easy to adapt, clear visibility, low risk and fast feedback loops.</p>

    </div>
  </li>
      
  
  
    
  
  <li class="c-reveal-block">
    <img class="c-reveal-block__icon js-showhide" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u1_s1_p3_reveal3.svg" alt="Kanban" width="266" height="266">
    
    <div class="c-reveal-block__desc">
      <h3 aria-hidden="true">Kanban</h3>
      <p>Kanban is a visual system and the flow of work is managed from using a Kanban project board.  Often these same types of project boards are used in Agile and Scrum methodology.</p>

    </div>
  </li>
      
  
  
    
  
  <li class="c-reveal-block">
    <img class="c-reveal-block__icon js-showhide" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u1_s1_p3_reveal4.svg" alt="Waterfall" width="266" height="266">
    
    <div class="c-reveal-block__desc">
      <h3 aria-hidden="true">Waterfall</h3>
      <p>Waterfall project management follows a chronological process and works on fixed dates, requirements, and outcomes. Its three main principles are low customer involvement, strong documentation, and sequential structure.</p>

    </div>
  </li>
  
</ul>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="agile-development" >
          
            <h2>Agile development</h2>
              <p>‘Agile Development’ which is also more commonly known as Agile Software Development, is an umbrella term for a range of frameworks, such as ‘Scrum’, and practices such as the ‘daily stand-up meeting’. It adopts the principles set out in the ‘Agile Manifesto’ and the ‘Agile 12 Principles’.</p>
<p>Agile came about as a process to address issues that software developers faced, focussing on a project being flexible, so it could respond to any changes and deal with any uncertainty.</p>
<p><img src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u1_s1_p3_wide_image_agile.svg" alt=""></p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
